<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">della Gherardesca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gherardesca family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The della Gherardesca were a powerful noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, counts of Donoratico in Tuscany. After the fall of the Judicate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> in 1258 they received a third of its lands in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, including the mining district around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Iglesias</a>. Their best-known member, Count Ugolino, appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s Inferno.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/della-gherardesca.jpg" alt="della Gherardesca" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_House_of_della_Gherardesca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Gherardesca was an ancient noble family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Republic of Pisa</a>, of Longobard origin, which probably dates back as early as the 11th century. It was one of the most prominent families first of Pisa, then of Volterra and eventually of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. Its members were of Ghibelline sympathies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>, who ruled Gallura on behalf of the Archdiocese of Pisa, may have been one of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> the family had a long rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-visconti">House of Visconti</a>, another Ghibelline family. In 1237 the Archbishop and the Emperor Frederick II intervened to reconcile the two houses, without success. The Gherardesca also took an interest in Pisa's affairs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. In 1230, when Ubaldo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>, a Visconti, invaded the Giudicato of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a>, they drove him back in the name of Benedetta and the young William II.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> was divided in 1258, the family received its south-western third. Around 1272 Count Ugolino della Gherardesca founded the mining town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Villa di Chiesa</a>, today Iglesias, in the Cixerri. A Gherardesca woman married John Visconti, Judge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>, who had received the north-eastern third, and the marriage finally reconciled the two families.</p><p>The family reached its height in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> under Ugolino in the 1270s and 1280s. He had to share power with his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nino-visconti">Nino Visconti</a>, but the two soon quarrelled, and the Pisans arrested Ugolino and deposed Nino from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>. After Ugolino's betrayal at the Battle of Meloria, the House of Orlandi also turned against the Gherardesca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The family survived the fall of Ugolino, who died in 1289, and produced churchmen and public figures in later centuries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a> della Gherardesca, duchess of Mantua, who died in 1349, the bishop Cosimo della Gherardesca and the archbishop Tommaso della Gherardesca. A Gherardesca Chapel stands in the church of Saint Francis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Della Gherardesca family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The judicate was divided among Pisa, Arborea, Gallura and the Pisan <b>della Gherardesca</b> family.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-cagliari">Judicate of Cagliari</a></li></ul></section>
